Selected Idea
-------------

Use the official GitHub CLI packages and repos as the default install path
The source repeatedly distinguishes recommended (official) installation methods - Debian/apt, RPM repos, Homebrew and precompiled binaries - and states these packages "are supported by the GitHub CLI maintainers" with updates "powered by GitHub CLI deployment workflow." The central editorial claim: prefer the official packages and repos published on cli.github.com as the primary installation and upgrade method for gh.

Research
--------

### Facts
1. The document contains a section titled "Recommended (Official)".
2. The document states: "Debian packages are hosted on the [GitHub CLI marketing site](https://cli.github.com/)".
3. The document states: "These packages are supported by the GitHub CLI maintainers with updates powered by [GitHub CLI deployment workflow](https://github.com/cli/cli/actions/workflows/deployment.yml)." (in the Debian section).
4. The document provides a multi-line shell command to install gh on Debian using apt.
5. The document provides apt commands to upgrade gh.
6. The document states: "RPM packages are hosted on the [GitHub CLI marketing site](https://cli.github.com)".
7. The document states: "These packages are supported by the GitHub CLI maintainers with updates powered by [GitHub CLI deployment workflow](https://github.com/cli/cli/actions/workflows/deployment.yml)." (in the RPM section).
8. The RPM section lists operating systems including Amazon Linux 2, CentOS, Fedora, Red Hat Enterprise Linux, openSUSE, and SUSE.
9. The document contains a DNF5 subsection that begins with a highlighted note: "These commands apply to DNF5 only.".
10. The document provides a DNF5 install command sequence for gh and a DNF5 upgrade command ("sudo dnf update gh").
11. The document contains a DNF4 subsection that begins with a highlighted note: "These commands apply to DNF4 only.".
12. The document provides a DNF4 install command sequence for gh and a DNF4 upgrade command ("sudo dnf update gh").
13. The document provides Amazon Linux 2 (yum) install and upgrade commands for gh.
14. The document provides openSUSE/SUSE Linux (zypper) install and upgrade commands for gh.
15. The document contains a Homebrew section and provides Homebrew install and upgrade commands: "brew install gh" and "brew upgrade gh".
16. The document states: "The [GitHub CLI formulae] is supported by the GitHub CLI maintainers with help from our friends at Homebrew with updated powered by [homebrew/hoomebrew-core](https://github.com/Homebrew/homebrew-core/blob/main/Formula/g/gh.rb)."
17. The document states: "[GitHub CLI releases](https://github.com/cli/cli/releases/latest) contain precompiled binaries for `386`, `amd64`, `arm64`, and `armv6` architectures."
18. The document contains a section titled "Community (Unofficial)".
19. The Community (Unofficial) section states: "The GitHub CLI team does not maintain the following packages or repositories. We are unable to provide support for these installation methods or any guarantees of stability, security, or availability for these installation methods.".
20. The Community section lists community-supported packaging channels including Alpine Linux, Android (Termux), Arch Linux, Conda, Fedora Community, Flox, FreeBSD, Funtoo, Gentoo, Manjaro, MidnightBSD, NetBSD/pkgsrc, Nix/NixOS, OpenBSD, openSUSE Tumbleweed, Solus, Spack, Void Linux, and Webi.
21. The document contains a "Discouraged" section with a warning that states: "The GitHub CLI team actively discourages use of the following methods of installation." and specifically references Snap.
22. The Discouraged / Snap subsection states: "The [GitHub CLI package](https://snapcraft.io/gh) has [so many issues with Snap](https://github.com/casperdcl/cli/issues/7) as a runtime mechanism for apps like GitHub CLI that our team suggests _never installing gh as a snap_.".
23. The Debian/apt section lists Raspberry Pi and Ubuntu Linux among the operating systems referenced in that context.

### Interpretations
1. [derived from Facts 1 and 18] The document separates installation methods into two groups labeled "Recommended (Official)" and "Community (Unofficial)".
2. [derived from Facts 2,3,6,7,16,17] The document identifies specific packaging channels (Debian/apt packages, RPM packages, Homebrew formulae, and precompiled binaries) and states those packages or formulae are supported by the GitHub CLI maintainers and have updates tied to specified workflows or repositories.

